                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran argues that the conventional wisdom of a strict inverse relationship between interest rates and stock prices is too simplistic. With the 10-year Treasury at 4.75% in 2026, he shows that markets have stayed resilient because strong earnings growthÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬ÂS\u0026P 500 analyst estimates up over 11% for both 2026 and 2027ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âhas more than offset the drag from higher discount rates. He demonstrates that rate sensitivity varies sharply by company type: high-growth, low-cash-flow businesses are hurt far more than mature, cash-generative ones.",
                                                              "take":  "Damodaran\u0027s framework cuts through the noise that dominates financial media every time rates tick up. The practical implication for investors: stop treating rate moves as a uniform sell signal for equities. The real question is always whether earnings growth is outrunning the discount rate increaseÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âand right now, for the broad market, it is. The danger is in the pockets of the market (speculative growth, long-duration tech) where earnings have not kept pace. Rate sensitivity is a stock-picker\u0027s variable, not a macro on/off switch.",
