                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran examines how venture capital\u0027s incentive structure systematically rewards revenue scaling over profitability, creating a structural bias toward broken business models. He argues that not all companies benefit from aggressive scalingÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Ânetwork effects and economies of scale are often assumed rather than proven. The proliferation of massive private funding rounds has allowed companies to delay their public market reckoning, hiding the underlying unit economics problem for years.",
                                                              "take":  "This is a timely warning for public market investors who assume IPO size equals business quality. The pattern Damodaran identifiesÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âscale now, profit neverÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âhas burned retail investors repeatedly in post-2021 IPO cohorts. The actionable takeaway: when evaluating any high-growth company, demand evidence of improving unit economics at scale, not just revenue growth. As the AI investment wave drives a new wave of startups toward IPO, this lens will matter more, not less.",
