                                                              "summary":  "Brown profiles Ron Baron\u0027s track record at Baron Capital, whose funds have outperformed the market for decades by taking concentrated, long-term positions in transformative companiesÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âmost famously Tesla and SpaceX. The Baron Partners Fund has beaten all major indices and peer funds since 2003 by holding exceptional businesses through extreme volatility and widespread investor skepticism. Baron\u0027s edge is not a formula but a disposition: deep conviction in visionary founders and the psychological discipline to not sell during drawdowns.",
                                                              "take":  "The Baron story is a useful corrective to the constant pressure to trade and rebalance. His returns validate a simple but psychologically brutal strategy: identify a handful of companies with genuine competitive moats, buy them, and do not sell when the narrative turns negative. The lesson for individual investors is not to clone Baron\u0027s positions but to audit their own holding periodsÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âmost underperformance comes from selling great businesses too early, not from picking bad ones. In an era of high-frequency news and social media volatility, Baron\u0027s multi-decade holding periods are a competitive advantage precisely because almost no one can replicate them emotionally.",
