                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran critiques venture capital\u0027s structural bias toward scaling revenue at the expense of building sustainable profitability. He argues that founder incentives, abundant capital access, and investor preferences for growth stories have created a class of \u0027Field of Nightmares\u0027 companies ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â massive in scale but without viable business models. Changes in both private and public markets, including public equity funds investing in private companies and weakened mean reversion in stock prices, have compounded the problem by extending the period before market discipline reasserts itself.",
                                                                                "take":  "This piece is quietly one of the most important warnings about the AI investment cycle. The same VC logic Damodaran dissects ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â scale first, figure out profits later ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â is being applied wholesale to AI startups, many of which are burning capital on inference costs and distribution while booking revenue that disguises margin-free or margin-negative economics. For any enterprise buyer evaluating AI vendor stability, the message is: probe the unit economics, not just the growth chart. Vendors that cannot articulate a path to positive contribution margins at current scale are flight risks within your planning horizon.",
