                                                                                "summary":  "Josh Brown interviews Ron Baron and his son Michael Baron of Baron Capital, examining how the fund grew from $10 million to approximately $70 billion in assets with over $60 billion returned to investors since its early days. The core of Baron\u0027s outperformance was early, concentrated conviction in Tesla ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â bought at $14 per split-adjusted share in 2014 and still held at a $5 billion position ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â and a $25 billion stake in SpaceX accumulated since 2017. Baron\u0027s strategy of holding concentrated positions through extreme volatility contrasts sharply with the risk management constraints that prevented institutional competitors from making similar bets. Baron predicts SpaceX could eventually reach a $20 trillion valuation as the world\u0027s most valuable company.",
                                                                                "take":  "The Baron story is a masterclass in the institutional constraints that systematically prevent most professional investors from capturing the biggest winners. Position size limits, style-box mandates, and benchmark-hugging all exist for defensible risk management reasons ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â but they collectively guarantee that the industry\u0027s median outcome excludes the outlier compounders. For individual investors, the lesson is uncomfortable: the path to exceptional long-term returns requires sitting through periods of unrealized loss on concentrated positions that most people ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â and all institutions ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â will not tolerate. Baron\u0027s SpaceX bet may prove to be the defining investment of the decade.",
